      "writtenOn": "2015-02-12T03:30:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No. Type-C pullup represents the MAX amount of current we can draw (and if it says 3A and changes to 1.5A we have to reduce our consumption in tSinkAdj). A proprietary method (or BC1.2 for the regular USB resistor) can override this. Donette will expect us to obey this rule!",
      "parentUuid": "16b5e5f7_7fa5ab93",
      "revId": "cf2e2401711d67907b6d4cb7f3636142cfe292b3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b66fb975_89cdb9bb",
        "filename": "board/samus_pd/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 670,
      "author": {
        "id": 1103909
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-02-12T05:18:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The problem is everything has a type-C pull-up, either 3A, 1.5A, or 500mA as I understand it. So, our A to C plug cable has a 500mA pull-up. Based on what you say, we should never ramp at all with BC1.2 devices because they are all going to have the 500mA pull-up.\n\nI guess there are two ways we could handle this:\n- Perhaps, the type-C supplier should be the LOWEST priority supplier, so that it only is used if none of the BC1.2 devices are detected. The problem is I\u0027m not convinced that the Pericom won\u0027t tell us there is a BC1.2 device when you plug in Donette, so we might have a problem with this.\n- We could say if supplier is type-C then only ramp if the type-C pull-up is for 500mA. Kinda ugly though.",
      "parentUuid": "f64db110_3b4e372a",
      "revId": "cf2e2401711d67907b6d4cb7f3636142cfe292b3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9664759b_88ab7722",
        "filename": "board/samus_pd/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 13
      },
      "lineNbr": 670,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000094
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-02-12T16:42:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I believe your second option to be the correct one.\n\nIf you see the USB pullup then all you know is regular USB rules are in effect (so 500mA if USB2 only, 900mA if USB3, the mess we address here if BC1.2). It isn\u0027t really a 500mA pullup, it just says to treat this like traditional USB. So completely correct to run our code here on seeing BC1.2. We will see this pullup on all converter cables that come from existing power adapters.\n\nThe spec is fairly clear that the 1.5A and 3A resistors take priority over USB rules -- if you see them you ignore the USB rules. (Which makes sense in a world where BC1.2 only goes up to the 1.5A of the standard). There is a section added that proprietary methods that give a current \u003e1.5A \u003c3A should use the 1.5A resistor, but someone detecting the proprietaty current level can use that (does not apply to standard USB detection only proprietary methods that tell you a current)\n\nFor a 3A/1.5A pullup we must obey the rule that permits an adapter to vary these (eg based on other devices plugged in to the adapter), so I don\u0027t think we can ever go above the resistor set limit and be in spec (and since by presenting the resistor the source guarantees the current is available we wouldn\u0027t want to set the limit lower). I also suspect that to allow working with non-typeC devices (i.e. with C plug-uB plug cables) adapters will present the DCP signature, which we should ignore because we understand the Type-C resistor.",
      "parentUuid": "b66fb975_89cdb9bb",
      "revId": "cf2e2401711d67907b6d4cb7f3636142cfe292b3",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
